}],"calendar":{"upcoming":[{"date":"2026-09-23","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-28","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-05","committee":"Cabinet","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-22","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-10-26","committee":"Governance and Business Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-09","committee":"Cabinet","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-25","committee":"Council","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-11-30","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-07-27","committee":"Audit Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-08-03","committee":"Cabinet","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-08-24","committee":"Governance and Business Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Cabinet","time":"4.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-17","committee":"Overview and Scrutiny Committee","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":true,"url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Calderdale is borrowing \u00a3151.4m (40% of capital programme) while managing structural revenue overspends","lead":"A council with a \u00a35.4m operating overspend and falling reserves is adding material borrowing costs to an already tight budget. The timing and interest rate assumption need to be clear.","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"First Quarter Projected Outturn - Revenue and Capital Update 2026-27","paper_url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s20194/20194.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Overall revenue overspend of \u00a35.36m (1.89%) confirmed at Q1 with only partial mitigation expected","lead":"A Q1 overspend of nearly \u00a35.4m with five months to run means the council is unlikely to deliver a balanced outturn without significant mid-year action. This is a governance and cash management issue.","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"First Quarter Projected Outturn - Revenue and Capital Update 2026-27","paper_url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s20194/20194.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"SEND High Needs deficit of \u00a330.5m will impose a \u00a311m liability on general balances from April 2028 when statutory override expires","lead":"A council with a known \u00a311m liability landing on its reserves in two years needs to demonstrate plans to address it. If general balances fall below prudent levels before then, the council will face a cash crisis.","iso":"2026-09-14","paper_title":"First Quarter Projected Outturn - Revenue and Capital Update 2026-27","paper_url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s20194/20194.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"First Quarter Projected Outturn - Revenue and Capital Update 2026-27","paper_url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s20194/20194.pdf","headline":"Calderdale is borrowing \u00a3151.4m (40% of capital programme) while managing structural revenue overspends","detail":"The council's capital programme totals \u00a3371.8m, with \u00a3151.4m (40%) to be funded by borrowing. Over 50% (\u00a3195.9m) comes from government and external grants. The paper notes it will review 'the anticipated timing of borrowing, aligned to delivery of the Capital Programme' but does not yet show what the revenue cost of this borrowing will be.","category":"borrowing","matters":"A council with a \u00a35.4m operating overspend and falling reserves is adding material borrowing costs to an already tight budget. The timing and interest rate assumption need to be clear.","figures":"\u00a3371.8m capital programme, \u00a3151.4m borrowing (40%), \u00a3195.9m grants (54%)","quote":"Over 50% of the Capital Programme is funded through external and government grants (\u00a3195.9m). Borrowing represents 40% of funding with the remainder of the programme funded from revenue contribution and pooled resources (capital receipts generated by the sale of assets).","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"First Quarter Projected Outturn - Revenue and Capital Update 2026-27","paper_url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s20194/20194.pdf","headline":"Overall revenue overspend of \u00a35.36m (1.89%) confirmed at Q1 with only partial mitigation expected","detail":"Calderdale approved a balanced budget of \u00a3282.96m but is forecasting year-end outturn of \u00a3288.5m, an overspend of \u00a35.36m. The paper indicates directorates are developing recovery plans but does not show them. The council is relying on a contribution to general balances of \u00a3680k, implying further reserve depletion.","category":"overspend","matters":"A Q1 overspend of nearly \u00a35.4m with five months to run means the council is unlikely to deliver a balanced outturn without significant mid-year action. This is a governance and cash management issue.","figures":"\u00a35.36m overspend (1.89%), budget \u00a3282.96m, forecast outturn \u00a3288.5m","quote":"There is currently a net overspend at year-end forecast on revenue budgets of \u00a35.36m or 1.89%. The report provides a detailed breakdown of the overspend across service areas and sets out action that is being taken to reduce the overspend by the 31 March 2027.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"First Quarter Projected Outturn - Revenue and Capital Update 2026-27","paper_url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s20194/20194.pdf","headline":"SEND High Needs deficit of \u00a330.5m will impose a \u00a311m liability on general balances from April 2028 when statutory override expires","detail":"Calderdale's SEND High Needs block is forecasting a \u00a330.5m deficit (costs of \u00a369m against DSG funding of \u00a338.5m). The statutory override allowing this deficit to sit on the balance sheet ends 31 March 2028. The DfE will fund 90% of accumulated deficits; the remaining 10% (estimated \u00a311m in 2028/29) must be funded from general balances, creating a known future cash demand.","category":"reserves","matters":"A council with a known \u00a311m liability landing on its reserves in two years needs to demonstrate plans to address it. If general balances fall below prudent levels before then, the council will face a cash crisis.","figures":"\u00a330.5m High Needs deficit for 2026/27, \u00a369m costs, \u00a338.5m DSG funding, \u00a311m estimated residual liability in 2028/29","quote":"The SEND High Needs budget was approved for 2026/27 with total costs estimated to be \u00a369.0m and funding from the Dedicated Schools Grant (DSG) of \u00a338.5m This resulted in a budgeted High Needs deficit of \u00a330.5m... The residual 10% will require funding from general balances and not Dedicated Schools Grant, this is estimated to be \u00a311m in 2028/29.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"First Quarter Projected Outturn - Revenue and Capital Update 2026-27","paper_url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s20194/20194.pdf","headline":"Children's Social Care experiencing \u00a31.57m pressure from complex, high-cost placements that are demand-led and cannot be reduced","detail":"Children's Social Care is forecasting a \u00a31.57m overspend driven by a small number of children with complex needs requiring specialised interventions and placements. Because the Council has a statutory duty to safeguard children in care and support care leavers, this spending is demand-led and cannot be controlled without breaching legal obligations.","category":"demand","matters":"Demand-led statutory spending that is growing faster than budgeted signals a cash constraint on a core service the council cannot rationally reduce. A lender needs to know whether this is structural or temporary.","figures":"\u00a31.57m forecast overspend within Children's Social Care budget","quote":"Children's Social Care continues to experience significant budgetary pressures arising from the increasing complexity of need presented by a small number of children requiring specialised interventions and placements... The combination of high-cost placements, increasing care package requirements, and rising costs associated with supporting care leavers has resulted in a forecast budget pressure of \u00a31.57m.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-14","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"First Quarter Projected Outturn - Revenue and Capital Update 2026-27","paper_url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s20194/20194.pdf","headline":"Adult Social Care overspend of \u00a34.6m with demand accelerating and savings not landing","detail":"Calderdale is forecasting a \u00a34.6m overspend in Adults and Wellbeing, its largest cost driver. Care home placements grew from 664 to 698 between February and August; Mental Health placements are up 31% and Learning Disability up 9%. Despite planned savings of \u00a32.56m, only \u00a3291k (11%) has been delivered so far.","category":"overspend","matters":"Adult social care is the hardest cost to control and signals structural budget weakness if demand and complexity continue. A lender would want to know how the council plans to arrest this or whether it expects sustained deficits.","figures":"\u00a34.6m overspend (4.5%), care homes 664\u2192698 placements, MH placements +31%, LD placements +9%, savings \u00a3291k of \u00a32.56m target (11% delivered)","quote":"The principal overspend drivers in Adults and Wellbeing are rising costs and volumes of care home placements, particularly Older People, Learning Disability and Mental Health placements. Growth in care home numbers from 664 to 698 people between February and August 2026. Mental Health care home placements increasing by 31%. Learning Disability care home placements increasing by 9%.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-14"},"name":"Calderdale","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-19","since_iso":"2026-08-20","distress":false,"headline":"Calderdale is borrowing \u00a3151.4m (40% of capital programme) while managing structural revenue overspends","lead":"A council with a \u00a35.4m operating overspend and falling reserves is adding material borrowing costs to an already tight budget. The timing and interest rate assumption need to be clear.","also":["4 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit Committee on 2026-09-28","n_findings":5,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"First Quarter Projected Outturn - Revenue and Capital Update 2026-27","top_paper_url":"https://calderdale.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s20194/20194.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":45,"news_drafts_total":3}}